elp take some of  the pressure off those waiting lists  at the same time as bringing in a  technology which is potentially much  more convenient for patients of course  they come through in a few days you have  to analyze the images that come off the  recorder that people wear when they take  the pill  but then they come through very quickly  indeed we have we have people trained to  read  the images that come from the capsule  they produce a report and those then  go back to the patient very quickly so  it's a it's a very  straightforward way of looking for  things like polyps in the bowel or early  cancers  and of course that's what we want is to  be able to catch cancer at an early  stage when it's most curable  um it's really just for cancers in the  bowel  it will of course examine the other  parts of the intestine as it passes  through and in fact i had a patient come  to me the other day  who'd had one of these capsules who had  a tumor in their small intestine which  is  which is much less common but generally  speaking the main thing we're using it  for is to look for bowel cancer  [Music]  [Applause]  this is a forgotten front line they are  dying here  boy we've got some interesting ways of  showing you what's going on their  message to us  get ready instructing you to stay at  home  i can't believe we did that it's pretty  special isn't it  i'm siobhan robbins sky's southeast asia  correspondent in bangkok  we take you to the heart of the stories  that shape our world  [Music]  hello welcome back to sophie ridge the  take this morning  with me jane secker well also on the  show this morning was the victims  commissioner dame vera beard i was keen  to get her view on the events last night  in clapham common the  organizers tried to cancel the clapham  common one  they failed completely because obviously  emotions are  pouring over the top  and and when it happens you know with  what looked like you were there i i  wasn't but it looked like thousands of  p